WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.750
Just an extraordinary thinker. Someone who continually reminded me that there was

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:12.000
another way of doing things, there was another way of thinking.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Things didn't have to be thought this way. He questioned everything.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.696
Roy Landau used to say that he'd question the most fixed assumptions in everyone

00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:26.000
else's minds

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.106
and almost mischievously go for the thing which was obviously unquestionable and

00:00:31.106 --> 00:00:32.000
questioned it.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
And it was lovely. Kept you alert.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
He was a sort of very sympathetic person. I mean, he cared about people.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.100
I mean, one of his tests when you went to the office for an interview apparently was

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.427
he'd ask you to do a drawing as an architect, but what he asked you to do was to

00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:49.860
draw two

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.600
escalators, one going up and one going down.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.937
And you had to make it clear which was which without resorting to anything obvious

00:00:55.937 --> 00:00:56.100
like

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.100
a sign or an arrow.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.100
It was very simple.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.100
You obviously put people on them.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.100
But if you didn't put people on, he'd say, you know, you're not an architect.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.560
You're not interested in people first and the escalator second.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.840
And it was a, this story may even be anecdotal, but it would be typical of Cedric.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.340
People first.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:18.420
And he really did care.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.100
And as a result, he wasn't really gentle with people who didn't.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.860
I think one of the most lovely things about Cedric was his sense of time.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.560
He was fascinated by time.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.060
He could always finish his lectures to the minute.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.840
He really cared about this.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.420
and when he, during this period when he was this permanent critic to my unit,

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:47.540
this is around about the very end of the 80s, let's say 89,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.660
Cedric and I were holding seminars on my yacht in St Catherine's Dock

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.100
very early every Friday morning,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.475
which was a kind of revenge on the students for the fact they never turned up for

00:01:59.475 --> 00:02:00.100
things on time.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.100
We had these meetings, I think they started at either 7 or 8,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.500
and there was champagne and caviar and smoked salmon

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.820
for those who got there on time.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:16.780
And they were fed and each of us in turn brought a guest for breakfast.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.520
And we brought every distinguished architect you can think of

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:26.180
plus a very eclectic mixture of politicians, left-wing unionists,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.720
Tory peers, a whole range of people came to the yacht for that.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.420
And it's all recorded in the logbook.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:37.580
At the end of the year, Cedric knew we were going to sail back to Ireland.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.200
And he asked rather casually what time high tide was.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.840
It was about 5am.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.180
The following morning, 5am, there is Cedric at the lock gates.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.621
Because Cedric of course knew that the only time he could get out was when the high

00:02:56.621 --> 00:02:58.860
tide, when the lock gates could open.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.157
and I thought it was a lovely story because it first of all showed his fixation with

00:03:03.157 --> 00:03:04.640
time and his interest in that

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.000
but also that he would bother.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.900
Couldn't he say goodbye the day before in the evening?

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.040
No, he had to come down to the, especially at five o'clock in the morning

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.820
which can't have been that convenient even for Cedric

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.860
and say goodbye.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.600
I mean, lovely.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:27.184
I mean, that kind of care, it's both compassion and care and consideration and

00:03:27.184 --> 00:03:27.700
timing.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:28.800
Perfect.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.900
I got a call one morning on the telephone, I think it was Christmas Eve,

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.880
and it was Nick Bailey on the phone, in fact.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.820
Nick was one of my students at Cambridge.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.180
Nick was working for Cedric at the time on the generator, it turned out,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.160
which I'd never heard at the time, saying,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.440
John, we need your help, and the story unfolded from there.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.220
Nick sent me, or Cedric, the following morning for Christmas present,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.160
this great wad of drawings appeared, which always made me laugh

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.200
because nowhere did it say what the site was.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.486
But the shadow projections were so accurate that I was able to work out it must be

00:03:59.486 --> 00:04:00.740
the latitude of Florida.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.920
Which tells you another thing about Cedric and accuracy and timing and so on.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.529
And also Nick, of course, is my student, so I knew it would be right, wouldn't it,

00:04:10.529 --> 00:04:10.780
Nick?

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:20.305
And the brief then was, could you write a computer program which would configure the

00:04:20.305 --> 00:04:20.720
site

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.660
in response to the changing needs of the client.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.780
And these all had strange names,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.440
like the rector, or I can't remember the names of the factor,

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.580
these people working on the site.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.020
The answer to that was yes.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.320
We had a Tektronix 4051,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.020
which I had managed to persuade the University of Elstertum by,

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.900
for me, as a kind of dowry,

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.440
to persuade me to go there.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.160
This must be the end of 1978.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:57.387
And we did just that. We wrote this software that enabled you to plan and replan the

00:04:57.387 --> 00:05:00.720
site in response to changing requirements.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.371
So you put those in from forms which CEDTRIQ had already been circulating to the

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:05.720
staff.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:09.720
And from that, the computer program would rework this out and plot it.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:14.862
We had the most sophisticated computing system in any university, in the Department

00:05:14.862 --> 00:05:16.720
of Architecture at that point,

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.360
which I had persuaded the then rector of the university there to buy as my diary.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:26.620
It was immensely expensive and we were probably the only people who could have

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.940
possibly done this for Cedric and so we wrote the software for him and

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.120
generated all these drawings. Now some interesting things about this, one of

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.260
which was Cedric never ever came to see the computer working nor did he

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:43.060
ever touch it himself but he sent Ron Heron and Ron Heron found this immensely

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.700
amusing because there's Ron with Archigram and you think all this

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:49.881
computer stuff, but a bit of it. Ron had never actually touched a computer in his

00:05:49.881 --> 00:05:50.500
life at this

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.966
point. Oh, Ron sits down there and thinks he's this great, but he'd only draw

00:05:55.966 --> 00:05:56.960
Cedric. And we

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.860
had this problem explaining to Ron that's because we'd programmed it. He said, well,

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.361
could you program it to draw Ron Herro? And I said, well, no, that's another

00:06:05.361 --> 00:06:05.820
project.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:12.175
And that was all wonderful. And then we, so we wrote this program for him that did

00:06:12.175 --> 00:06:12.860
everything

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:20.497
he wanted. And then we had a little think about this and Julia and I, my wife was

00:06:20.497 --> 00:06:20.780
the

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:26.070
partner working on this project, she did most of the programming, I did most of the

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:26.780
systems design.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:32.421
We decided that the problem, one of the problems was you'd never know whether the

00:06:32.421 --> 00:06:33.020
crane driver

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:38.310
had moved the parts to the right place and how would the program know where. So at

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:39.020
that point,

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:41.140
this was 1978, at that point,

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.080
the very first microprocessor-based computer chips

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.100
appeared on the market.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.880
One of them was in this Tektronix 4051 we had.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.920
So we had one of the very first ever into England.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:55.300
Well, actually, it was Ireland.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:59.480
And he said, right, we'll embed one of those

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.060
in every single part of the building

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.200
so the building is literally intelligent and knows where it is.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:06.960
And to prove this, we built a model, a physical model,

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.792
which is a red gridded board which is now in the Museum of Modern Art and each cell

00:07:11.792 --> 00:07:12.200
of this

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:17.555
represented one cell of the building and that contained every single part contained

00:07:17.555 --> 00:07:18.200
a computer

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:23.601
chip and these computers communicated with each other and they drove a plotter which

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:24.180
then drew

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:29.142
the overall configuration of the site but then they became the controlling computer

00:07:29.142 --> 00:07:29.620
and they

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.909
talked to each other and decided how to best configure the site so the building

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:34.560
became quite

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.470
literally intelligent, a phrase which Dan Sudjik picked up in the, I think it was

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:40.440
the RIBA journal

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.907
at the time. Anyhow, the point about this was that there was too much computing

00:07:45.907 --> 00:07:47.360
power. So what do you

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.107
do when you've got too much brain power? Well, you get bored. So we thought, right,

00:07:51.107 --> 00:07:51.920
the system can get

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:57.638
bored with itself and start making arbitrary suggestions. And this is not facetious

00:07:57.638 --> 00:07:58.120
because

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:04.663
the point about that was it would provoke people to make changes. If a cell of the

00:08:04.663 --> 00:08:05.620
building got

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:09.470
bored with where it was and sent instructions to the crane driver to move it, the

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:10.040
crane driver

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:14.155
would never know why, whether this is because the cell was bored or because there

00:08:14.155 --> 00:08:15.120
was some real need.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.461
But the result of doing this is they would provoke a chain reaction. So first of

00:08:19.461 --> 00:08:19.660
all,

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:24.660
people get used to changing buildings. Secondly, that on the basis of these

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:25.860
arbitrary changes,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.580
there would be feedback so it could start to learn.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.660
And that was the most important thing.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.460
Not that the building was intelligent,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.200
but the building was intelligent enough to learn from the response to people

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.180
how to better rearrange itself.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:42.680
With some sort of idealistic vision that maybe in the end

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:44.520
it would get better at organising itself

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.440
than we might be able to organise it, having set this process in.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.920
So we built this working model.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.900
And I flew over to London with it to show Cedric,

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:55.420
and he was so excited.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.940
because we couldn't carry the computer,

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.000
so he went and hired a Commodore PET,

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.020
personal electronic transactor.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.640
That's what we had before PCs.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:06.480
Personal electronic trans...

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.980
And he hired one at the top of the call road.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:09.480
And there it was in his office,

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.060
and he was expecting massive hassles settling out.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:12.880
He said, no, no, no, Cedric,

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:14.820
it's going to take me ten minutes to wire this up.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:15.760
So he said, well, have a brandy.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:16.840
So they were dreaming.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.000
And we plug all this together, and it worked.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:19.800
Lo and behold.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:21.120
And there Cedric...

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:21.840
Absolutely.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.160
I've never seen Cedric totally gobsmacked

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:27.734
As he found he could move this model around, as he moved it around, the computer

00:09:27.734 --> 00:09:28.360
screen changed

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.877
and it produced perspectives of each of the permutations as fast as he could change

00:09:32.877 --> 00:09:33.040
it.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:40.196
And that was the only time I ever saw Cedric touch anything remotely like a computer

00:09:40.196 --> 00:09:40.820
himself.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.060
But he loved it.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:49.276
And that was how we came to make the generator, or propose that the generator should

00:09:49.276 --> 00:09:50.300
be intelligent.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:55.560
This is exactly what he said in Building Design, January 1979.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.580
Just to add to the computer and architecture debate,

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.780
I include this proposal from my consultants

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:07.580
on our latest project in which this friendly machine is widely used.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:10.840
Oh yes, the site and the elements on it

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.280
should have a life and intelligence of their own

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.340
and the programme would start to generate unsolicited plans,

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:19.300
improvements and modifications

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.160
in response to users, comments, records of activity,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.020
or even by building in a boredom concept,

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.880
so the site itself starts to make proposals

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.100
about changes of itself if no changes are made.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.780
Now, such a proposal can only work

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.520
if the initial design allows layouts and structures

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.800
that can alter and decay and disappear.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.100
Mine can, et cetera.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.060
So he got it, he understood it,

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.540
He wrote about it. That's enough.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:57.200
Monica Pidgeon published a series of audio tapes about projects at the time,

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:58.500
and one was The Generator.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:00.680
And he prefaces it.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:02.680
The first slide that comes up is,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.520
technology is the answer, but what was the question?

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:09.600
And I think this was right,

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.560
that Sedgwick realised how very, very powerful the computer could be,

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.020
but how it wasn't going quite in the direction it ought to.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:22.780
You asked me a moment ago about Cedric's understanding of computers and the attitude

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:28.780
of the technology. I make two observations about that. One is specifically about the

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:34.156
computer, the other is about technology in general. The example I made a note about

00:11:34.156 --> 00:11:34.480
here,

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.160
which I'll read because it will make it clearer for me, which I wrote before, but

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:42.433
he said, whilst Cedric was always the first to understand the potential of a new

00:11:42.433 --> 00:11:43.020
technology,

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.420
and I think that exactly describes it.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:47.620
He was very technologically alert.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:52.020
He was always producing clippings from the journals about technology.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:56.020
He was far too sensible to actually use it.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:58.020
He left that to others.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:00.020
Hence, no voice messages.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:03.020
He didn't have a tape in his voice machine.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:08.020
He didn't want to waste the morning dealing with yesterday's problems, as he put it.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.020
And also, he had no fax machine,

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:15.920
although later I discovered he did, but it had no paper in it for the same reason.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.880
No word processor, no computer drafting.

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.620
So technology could be just a thought experiment

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:26.500
and what we came to describe later as computing without computers.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.560
And the other observation about this later, when he was teaching with me,

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:37.740
I picked up the brief on the way here for a project he set in 1990 with me.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.405
and Cedric participated in one of a series of introductory exercises called Choice

00:12:43.405 --> 00:12:45.340
with my AA diploma students.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:50.500
The brief began with a quote from Cedric.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:56.200
Taking infinite pains with a problem is best left to the computer.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.260
Making a choice is the human's role.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:07.080
This exercise will test your thinking so far by looking at the generator project.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.867
you'll be required to hypothesise rules and procedures for organising the generator

00:13:11.867 --> 00:13:12.040
and

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:16.996
simulate the results with models. Propose how the computer might be useful or

00:13:16.996 --> 00:13:17.640
otherwise,

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:25.570
but try to refrain from using it. Cedric's sense of humour was an absolute delight

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:26.760
to those of us

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:32.835
who were on his wavelength. His humour was magic. It was that beautiful, dry, wry

00:13:32.835 --> 00:13:33.960
English humour.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.520
where sometimes you didn't even realise he'd made a joke until a split second later.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:44.360
The danger with this is it doesn't transfer well to other cultures.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:45.600
It doesn't translate.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.940
In fact, it really makes some people very angry

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.380
because they confuse it with sarcasm,

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:54.640
or they think it's akin to irony,

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:57.620
and that's greatly hated, particularly in America,

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.120
and particularly into cultures where it won't work,

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:01.460
like many Asian cultures.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.020
you just can't translate ironic humour.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.920
So I think that Cedric may have also been the victim of his humour

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.800
in the sense that I suspect a large number of people

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.060
either couldn't understand what he was talking about

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:19.080
or misunderstood it or maybe were even angered by it.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:22.620
And that may be part of the reason why he's such an enigma

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:26.220
and doesn't have a higher international regard as he might have,

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:27.520
particularly in America.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:33.833
I think the iconic Cedric project has to be the first major project in the Fun

00:14:33.833 --> 00:14:34.400
Palace.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.460
For several reasons. First, it was early, of course.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.340
Secondly, it has a cute name.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:47.335
Thirdly, it somehow was the first building to ever embody critical ideas about

00:14:47.335 --> 00:14:49.660
flexibility, interaction,

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:53.500
the idea that the people using the building could control it,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:58.300
that it might need some cybernetic system indeed to control it.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:02.960
When the press wanted drawings and things, there were no elevations.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:07.180
They finally got a kind of helicopter view of it at night

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:10.380
with this sort of structural skeleton which explained almost nothing.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.020
And then there were some enigmatic little diagrams

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.960
with captions sort of like west elevation, north elevation.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:19.120
They said movement, flow, direction.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:24.080
and it was all in black and white and cut out.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.300
This all came from the impresario,

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.440
Joan Littlewood, the theatre impresario,

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:34.060
and she was a friend of Gordon Pask's, the cybernetician,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.580
because he had an interest in theatre.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:42.000
And the other reason it has to be the most important

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:46.160
and earliest building is that it was so obviously copied.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:51.910
and the most obvious one being the Pompidou Center where a lot of the notion of the

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:52.880
big spaces the

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:57.880
intended flexibility were there and the original competition proposals had a lot

00:15:57.880 --> 00:15:58.880
more flexibility

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:06.166
and moving parts than ever finally constructed so yes wonderful to see it but sad it

00:16:06.166 --> 00:16:06.860
was kind

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:11.954
of monumental eyes I mean I love Richard Rogers and I love the Pompidou Center but

00:16:11.954 --> 00:16:12.700
on the other

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:18.881
hand it kind of, by monumentalising the Fun Palace, it killed it. There was no real

00:16:18.881 --> 00:16:19.700
interaction

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:27.342
there, there is no real flexibility, but it's obviously a very different kind of

00:16:27.342 --> 00:16:27.980
exhibition

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:33.911
space and it looks very different and everyone remembers it because all those pipes

00:16:33.911 --> 00:16:34.340
on the

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:40.640
outside. It gained notoriety and interest for the wrong reasons and, that's my point

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:47.286
there and distracted interest from the flexibility and potential of a very different

00:16:47.286 --> 00:16:47.840
kind of

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:53.920
gallery which is what the underlying concept of the Fun Palace was. And that

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:54.960
building I've

00:16:54.960 --> 00:17:00.879
seen it again and again and again and again, acknowledged or otherwise ideas. Some

00:17:00.879 --> 00:17:01.240
cases

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:08.291
students don't even know where the ideas have come from because somehow we don't

00:17:08.291 --> 00:17:08.820
have a

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:15.263
a very good history of the immediate past. I love the Potteries Thinkbelt project

00:17:15.263 --> 00:17:15.820
because

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:23.920
it rethinks what universities might be and addressed really serious social issues in

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:31.887
the central area of Britain which had been the potteries and which had been totally

00:17:31.887 --> 00:17:32.560
destroyed

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:42.464
by rises in automation of pottery fabrication. The university gathers monastic-like

00:17:42.464 --> 00:17:43.180
groups

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:49.759
round the only really valuable resource which is rare old books. And 800 years ago

00:17:49.759 --> 00:17:50.080
this

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:55.660
made a lot of sense because all books were rare and precious and these centres of

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:56.280
knowledge

00:17:56.280 --> 00:18:01.588
and those monks who looked after them in these orders who had the discipline to

00:18:01.588 --> 00:18:02.260
discipline

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:09.632
the minds to study these works made some kind of sense 800 years ago perhaps. But

00:18:09.632 --> 00:18:10.360
now what

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.808
kind of sense does that make? We don't need central libraries anymore and

00:18:14.808 --> 00:18:15.600
universities.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:20.400
We don't need lectures anymore in the sense of everyone sitting around and being

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:21.120
pontificated

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:28.226
to by some professor like me. It's a nonsense. So Cedric really addressed this issue

00:18:28.226 --> 00:18:28.480
and

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:36.046
produced this proposal to use the railway network in an industrially running down

00:18:36.046 --> 00:18:36.420
area

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:42.962
to connect people up, to make this open to people who had missed out on university

00:18:42.962 --> 00:18:43.760
education,

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:52.539
an alternative if you like to the open university. And it was a kind of iconic

00:18:52.539 --> 00:18:54.340
statement of how

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:59.300
architecture could address social issues. The problem was always raised that

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:04.340
Sedgwick was quite clearly interested in things being more than objects as

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:08.260
architecture, but how did this manifest itself? Well in this case it manifested

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:12.160
itself as a series of railway trucks which variously unfolded or whatever.

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:15.520
The project when looked at in detail doesn't actually make a great deal of

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:20.880
sense but no matter because it stands as an iconic idea of an alternative, a

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:25.980
flexible thing in a rundown area and architecture being the proactive force

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:32.560
of change to address these issues so I have a great interest. It's also the

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:37.440
project that he chose to show at the International Dialogue of Experimental

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:44.400
Architecture at Folkestone in 1966. This was a crucial year for me.

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:51.400
This conference in Folkestone was supposed to be a small, basically

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:57.840
archigram based type event in a little coastal town which Peter Cook loved

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:03.960
and it got totally out of hand. All over Europe thousands of architectural

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:08.280
students turned up so expecting an audience of 300 there were 3,000

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:15.594
sleeping on the beach. And there were the most fantastic talks and it was chaotic.

00:20:15.594 --> 00:20:16.040
There

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:20.947
was light getting into the lecture theatre, you couldn't see Cedric's slides.

00:20:20.947 --> 00:20:21.520
Archigram

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:25.476
got booed but nothing light to the extent that Claude Parra and Paul Virilio with

00:20:25.476 --> 00:20:25.720
their

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:30.068
architecture pranquit, they were just shouted off the stage and booed because they

00:20:30.068 --> 00:20:30.280
were

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:36.694
seen as being neo-fascist. The atmosphere was fabulous. It was all happening at

00:20:36.694 --> 00:20:37.100
night

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:45.325
on the beaches with everyone talking and exchanging ideas in a way which I had never

00:20:45.325 --> 00:20:46.500
seen before.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:51.367
And that is when I first heard Cedric talk about the Finkbelts Proterites and I

00:20:51.367 --> 00:20:51.860
thought,

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:57.646
my god, this scheme is the most revolutionary project of everything we've seen in

00:20:57.646 --> 00:20:58.860
this mind-blowing

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:04.737
conference. He used to turn up to schools of architecture conferences and things,

00:21:04.737 --> 00:21:05.100
which

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.740
It's just been very dull for him, but I think he saw this as part of his duty.

00:21:09.300 --> 00:21:10.880
He had something called polyarch.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:13.820
In those days, the polytechnics were differentiated from universities,

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:17.860
and the polytechnics, the schools of architecture, were so boring.

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.460
The projects were boring, the staff were boring.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:24.520
They were frustrated by the...

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:27.760
The students and the staff were frustrated by the projects

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:30.160
and the principals of the colleges,

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:34.320
who were generally career administrators

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:39.420
rather than enlightened and charismatic academics

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.620
or charismatic professional architects.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:45.740
And he set up this thing called PolyArch.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:50.900
The idea was to travel around and try and help some of these destitute.

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:56.120
And of course the schools thought of this as gross arrogance on Cedric's part

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.760
and the students absolutely adored it.

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:01.500
And after it became a little bit politically problematic

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.420
to actually go to the universities,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:09.420
I'm thinking of the Cedric Price Memorial event where Rem and I both spoke.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:14.996
Rem and I had a bit of a frosty relationship previously, but of course we shook

00:22:14.996 --> 00:22:15.420
hands,

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:20.063
and polite as one does on such occasions, but Rem said he couldn't stay for my

00:22:20.063 --> 00:22:20.420
piece.

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:27.420
So he went first, and he really was quite generous about Cedric,

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:34.100
And you could see that Cedric was a real nudge to his conscience.

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:39.847
When Peter Cook described Cedric as the conscience of us all, you could see this was

00:22:39.847 --> 00:22:40.600
troublesome

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:42.240
to Rem.

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:48.216
And Rem had to kind of nail this, so he talked about the Tensegrity cage, the bird

00:22:48.216 --> 00:22:48.580
cage,

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:53.280
and the only thing, but it was still a cage.

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:57.300
That was sort of Rem coping with it.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:04.574
And then I talked and I had, because you imagine quite a different spin on all this,

00:23:04.574 --> 00:23:05.180
and Rem

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:11.462
stayed and Rem came up afterwards and we shook hands properly and we both said we

00:23:11.462 --> 00:23:12.160
respected

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:15.040
what each other had said.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:22.682
But I think that is a sort of indicator of the problem that those with a formal

00:23:22.682 --> 00:23:23.940
preoccupation

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:24.940
have.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:28.980
And Rem would like to think he's more than that and beyond that, I'm sure.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:33.460
But it keeps on manifesting itself in such formal ways.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:37.480
Whereas with Cedric, the form never gets in the way.

00:23:38.180 --> 00:23:40.840
It's all to do, the process is so visible,

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:44.600
to the extent that sometimes nothing got built at all.

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:49.180
Roy was a close friend of Cedric's, and Roy was my third-year tutor,

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:51.540
and I knew them both very well.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:55.620
They knew each other very well and dined together frequently at the Gay Hussar.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:24:01.680
And the way Roy put it was, the questioning of indisputable premises.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:08.903
Another quote from Cedric himself was that architecture should have little to do

00:24:08.903 --> 00:24:10.180
with problem solving.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:18.112
Rather, it should create desirable conditions and opportunities, hitherto thought

00:24:18.112 --> 00:24:19.040
impossible.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:25.856
I like that. There's no point in asking for the brief because how would anyone know

00:24:25.856 --> 00:24:27.580
what they would want?

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:36.440
He's going to offer them something they didn't know they could have.

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:44.196
And actually, I've only just been to see the Tugendhat house in Bruno, the Mies

00:24:44.196 --> 00:24:44.820
building.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:51.780
And Mies said an almost extraordinarily similar thing to his clients.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:57.017
And I thought to myself, I could have imagined that Mies and Cedric might have

00:24:57.017 --> 00:24:58.360
something in common.

00:24:58.360 --> 00:25:01.220
And then I remembered the cigars.

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:11.470
I'm not being silly. There's something about the way they sat and the photographs of

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:12.080
them.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:18.780
There's one picture of Mies exhaling cigar smoke in the book I've just got,

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:22.760
and there's one of Cedric, and they could be transposable.

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:26.760
Maybe smoking cigars does something to the...

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:31.740
Anyway, Price was the most provocative questioner of the architectural profession

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:37.220
and its assumptions, rejecting traditional architectural conventions and values,

00:25:37.740 --> 00:25:40.880
challenging the social, functional, aesthetic norms.

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:45.500
Price was extremely highly regarded and acknowledged by the leading architectural

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:51.116
thinkers of the time but remained an enigma to the rest of a sleepy profession, as

00:25:51.116 --> 00:25:52.280
Cedric called it.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:57.927
The catalogue of projects from his small practice is immense in quantity as well as

00:25:57.927 --> 00:25:58.880
the quality of

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:05.652
their thinking but most of all was his staggering ability to think the unimaginable.

00:26:05.652 --> 00:26:06.620
His projects

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:11.360
are recognised as the seminal architectural propositions of the 20th century.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:15.740
I'm quoting myself here from a book called The New Makers of Modern Culture.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.640
If you don't know this book, it's rather special

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:22.960
because it's not an encyclopaedia of the great worthies of the 20th century.

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:27.620
Everyone being invited to make a contribution,

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:31.720
it wasn't confirmed that you would make the contribution

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:38.300
until you wrote explaining why they were a maker of modern culture.

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:42.440
That is, it was no good just to have been important,

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:46.740
because you can think of any number of architects who didn't get into the book.

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:52.660
Now, Cedric got in because I made the case that he was a contributor,

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:57.860
in the sense that he made aspects of culture in the 20th century.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:26:59.780
And I think that's absolutely true.

00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:07.979
And that's why I think his reputation will survive, because he was a maker of modern

00:27:07.979 --> 00:27:08.760
culture.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:15.738
His ability to rethink every issue, to approach everything fresh, to approach

00:27:15.738 --> 00:27:20.420
everything as if it had never been thought about before,

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:25.740
but at the same time respecting history and previous ideas.

00:27:25.740 --> 00:27:31.051
Cedric's library is of great interest. Eleanor Bronn and Sam Harding published after

00:27:31.051 --> 00:27:31.620
his death

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:38.448
a complete catalogue to the library and seeing the eclecticism and the Catholicism

00:27:38.448 --> 00:27:39.780
of that reading,

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:44.955
including eight copies no less of the Pickwick papers, really starts to give

00:27:44.955 --> 00:27:45.840
insights into

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:52.469
Cedric's thinking, his humanitarianism, his breadth of scientific architectural

00:27:52.469 --> 00:27:53.140
research

00:27:53.140 --> 00:28:01.340
and his basic delving into the making of culture as we understand it.

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:06.571
The fundamental concepts of the generator and the fun palace and the Potteries

00:28:06.571 --> 00:28:07.080
Thinkbelt

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:11.020
are models of a future architecture as yet unrealised.

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:15.943
These are not so much projects but generic ideas about new ways of making

00:28:15.943 --> 00:28:16.640
environments

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:19.840
responsive to the needs and desires of their users.

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:26.177
They represent a fundamental archetype of a new deal for users, or a new menu in

00:28:26.177 --> 00:28:27.840
Cedric's terminology.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:32.538
The generator still has much to say to those who believe that architecture should

00:28:32.538 --> 00:28:33.340
serve the users,

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:38.758
and a new model might speak to a new generation who might yet realise the future of

00:28:38.758 --> 00:28:40.900
which Cedric has given us a vision.
